From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: offer the picture settings as V4L2 controls

The picture pipeline of this chip belongs to the MIPS co-processor, so
every picture setting it has is a THal_Vp_* remote procedure and not a
register the ARM could write. That has meant a private tool so far: the
values were reachable only by sending RPCs over /dev/cpu_comm by hand, and
nothing that looks for picture settings where V4L2 keeps them -- v4l2-ctl,
qv4l2, any ordinary application -- could find them at all.

Offer them on /dev/videoN, where the pictures come out.

Nine of them. Brightness, contrast, saturation, hue and sharpness take the
standard ids. The two noise reductions, the dynamic contrast and the black
extension have no standard id in V4L2 at all, so they take ids from a new
user-class block for this driver; their four steps and the names of those
steps are the vendor's own, out of the header of pq_picturemode.ini
("0-off,1-low,2-middle,3-high"), which is also where the 0..100 range of
the other five comes from.

That these RPCs reach *our* picture is measured, not assumed. SetContrast
0 -> 100 moves 12.16 % of the pixels on the wall and the smaller step
20 -> 80 moves 0.84 % of them, on the picture this driver captures
(doku/nachtlog/K5-board-verifikation.md (c) and (d)).

No value is converted on the way in. Each of the five settings measured on
the device lands unchanged in its field of the firmware's register block --
SetContrast 20/80/100 gives 0x14/0x50/0x64 in 0x05001234[31:16] -- so the
V4L2 range is mapped onto the RPC argument and onto nothing else.
Saturation shows why that is the right place to stop: the same RPC also
writes a second register, the chroma gain at 0x05140508[23:16], as
floor(argument * 1.28), and a driver that computed a register value itself
would get that half wrong.

The controls start at the values the firmware reports, not at values picked
here. After the bring-up sequence the driver asks each setting's Get
routine once and uses the answer as the control's value and as its default.
That is what makes a truthful control possible without inventing a number:
announcing 50 while the firmware holds something else is a lie, and writing
50 to make the announcement true would move a picture nobody asked to move
-- for saturation it would take the chroma gain with it. An answer outside
the range, or none at all, falls back to the vendor's "standard" preset and
says so in the log.

The readback checks itself. The bring-up sequence has just set the temporal
and spatial noise reduction, the dynamic contrast and the black extension
to known values, and the driver compares its four answers against them --
reading the expected values out of the sequence table rather than writing
them down a second time, so the two cannot drift apart. Four agreements are
evidence that the Get routines are read correctly; a disagreement is a
warning in the log and means this patch's reading of them is wrong.

Both call shapes come from the decompiled handlers
(re/captures/weltneuheit/k5-pq-handler-20260907.log): a Set handler reads
its value out of the first argument word and touches nothing else, a Get
handler takes no argument at all and answers with exactly one word, the
value. That matters for more than convenience -- SetGamma, SetWhiteBalance
and SetColorManagement take a *pointer* that the firmware dereferences
without checking it, the failure mode that takes the co-processor down when
THal_Vp_Init is handed a zero staging address. None of those three is
offered here.

Nothing is offered twice either. Gamma and white balance are already
GAMMA_LUT and CTM on the display CRTC, where they are measured to invert
the picture on the console and on the video path alike, while the firmware
announces "Can not get MP GAMMAModuleID" for its own gamma module at every
start; the lamp already has pwm-backlight and a backlight class device to
go with it. Picture mode, video range and low latency mode are left out for
reasons the code names one by one -- in short, because their values cannot
be named from anything the vendor ships, and a menu whose entries cannot be
named is worse than no menu.

Brightness was the doubtful one of the nine and is not doubtful any more.
It writes its register -- SetBrightness 100 gives 0x00000064 in
0x05001234[15:0] -- but the run that looked for an effect on the wall saw
0.00 % of the pixels move, where the same step in contrast moved 12.16 %,
and the conclusion drawn from that was to leave the control out. The run
went against a nearly white page, and brightness moves the lower end of the
curve: its criterion could not have succeeded whatever the hardware did.
Re-measured against dark material: the standard deviation in the region of
interest goes 12.6 -> 16.2 -> 22.7 for SetBrightness 0 -> 50 -> 100 and
then stays flat through 150, 200 and 255, and stepping back and forth
lands on the same three numbers again
(doku/nachtlog/I0-helligkeit-nachgemessen.md). So brightness works -- and
its range really is 0..100, which is now measured and not only read off
the vendor file.

v4l2_ctrl_handler_setup() is deliberately not called: it would write all
nine values into the firmware during probe, which is exactly what the
readback exists to avoid.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:00:00.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:30:00.000000000 +0200
@@ -69,12 +69,13 @@
 
 #include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h>
 #include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-arisc.h>
 #include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-cpu-comm.h>
 
 #include <media/v4l2-common.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-ctrls.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-dev.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-device.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-event.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-fh.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-ioctl.h>
@@ -334,12 +335,217 @@
 		.name = "THal_Vp_HDMI_SetHPDTimeInterval",
 		.kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN, .nargs = 1, .args = { 0xc8 },
 		.session = 33, .why = "0xc8 = 200 ms.",
 	},
 };
 
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
+ * Picture quality
+ *
+ * The picture pipeline belongs to the firmware, so every picture setting
+ * this chip has is an RPC and not a register this driver could write. They
+ * are offered here as V4L2 controls, on the node the pictures come out of,
+ * so that v4l2-ctl, qv4l2 and any ordinary application find them where they
+ * look for them instead of needing a private tool for each one.
+ *
+ * That the stage these RPCs drive really sits in the path of *our* picture
+ * is measured, not assumed: SetContrast 0 -> 100 moves 12.16 % of the pixels
+ * on the wall, against 0.84 % for the smaller step 20 -> 80
+ * (doku/nachtlog/K5-board-verifikation.md (c) and (d)).
+ *
+ * The argument of every one of these RPCs is the value itself. Each of the
+ * five that were measured on the device lands unchanged in its field of the
+ * firmware's picture-quality register block -- SetContrast 20/80/100 gives
+ * 0x14/0x50/0x64 in 0x05001234[31:16] and nothing in between rescales it
+ * (doku/81-pq-datenmodell.md section 3). So no conversion belongs here: the
+ * V4L2 range is mapped onto the RPC argument, and what the firmware then
+ * does with it is the firmware's business. Saturation shows why that
+ * matters -- the same RPC also writes a second register, the chroma gain at
+ * 0x05140508[23:16], as floor(argument * 1.28); a driver that computed a
+ * register value itself would get that half wrong (measured, five points
+ * including the pair 59/60 that separates rounding down from rounding to
+ * nearest, doku/nachtlog/K5-board-verifikation.md (f)).
+ *
+ * Five of the nine have a standard control id. The other four -- the two
+ * noise reductions, the dynamic contrast and the black extension -- have no
+ * standard id in V4L2 at all, so they take ids from this driver's own block
+ * in the user class. Their four steps and the names of those steps are not
+ * invented: they are the vendor's own, written out in the header of
+ * pq_picturemode.ini ("0-off,1-low,2-middle,3-high"), which is also where
+ * the 0..100 range of the other five comes from.
+ *
+ * Deliberately absent, each for its own reason:
+ *
+ *  - Gamma and white balance. Both exist twice on this chip, and the half
+ *    that is known to work is not this one: the DE2 gamma stage is offered
+ *    as GAMMA_LUT and CTM on the display CRTC (patch 0095) and is measured
+ *    to invert the picture on the console and on the video path alike
+ *    (doku/87-gamma-ctm-kms.md section 0), while the firmware announces
+ *    "Can not get MP GAMMAModuleID" for its own gamma module at every
+ *    start. A V4L2_CID_GAMMA here would be a second owner of one visible
+ *    quantity with no defined precedence between them.
+ *
+ *  - The backlight. The lamp already has a driver, pwm-backlight on
+ *    PWM2/PB4, and a class device to go with it. Whether
+ *    Thal_Vp_SetBacklightLevel drives that same lamp is not measured, and
+ *    two unrelated owners of one lamp are worse than one.
+ *
+ *  - Picture mode. SetPictureMode loads a whole preset and re-sets the five
+ *    values above behind this driver's back (doku/85-re-pq-register.md
+ *    A.5), so a control for it would silently invalidate five others; and
+ *    the firmware's numbering of the modes cannot be resolved from any file
+ *    the vendor ships. A menu whose entries cannot be named is worse than
+ *    no menu.
+ *
+ *  - Video range and low latency mode. Both are settable, but nothing in
+ *    the vendor data says what their values mean, not even how many there
+ *    are. G_FMT answers V4L2_QUANTIZATION_LIM_RANGE; a range control that
+ *    could not say which of its values matches that answer would make the
+ *    node contradict itself.
+ *
+ *  - SetGamma, SetWhiteBalance and SetColorManagement. Their argument is a
+ *    *pointer*, which the handler dereferences without checking it -- the
+ *    failure mode that takes the co-processor down when THal_Vp_Init gets a
+ *    zero staging address. Visible in the handler decompilations,
+ *    re/captures/weltneuheit/k5-pq-handler-20260907.log.
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
+
+/*
+ * Private control ids. The base is registered in the uapi header so that no
+ * other driver takes the same block.
+ */
+#define V4L2_CID_H713_TEMPORAL_NR	(V4L2_CID_USER_SUN50I_H713_BASE + 0)
+#define V4L2_CID_H713_SPATIAL_NR	(V4L2_CID_USER_SUN50I_H713_BASE + 1)
+#define V4L2_CID_H713_DYNAMIC_CONTRAST	(V4L2_CID_USER_SUN50I_H713_BASE + 2)
+#define V4L2_CID_H713_BLACK_EXTENSION	(V4L2_CID_USER_SUN50I_H713_BASE + 3)
+
+/* The vendor's own four steps, from the header of pq_picturemode.ini. */
+static const char * const h713_hdmirx_pq_steps[] = {
+	"Off", "Low", "Middle", "High", NULL,
+};
+
+enum h713_hdmirx_pq_index {
+	H713_PQ_BRIGHTNESS,
+	H713_PQ_CONTRAST,
+	H713_PQ_SATURATION,
+	H713_PQ_HUE,
+	H713_PQ_SHARPNESS,
+	H713_PQ_TNR,
+	H713_PQ_SNR,
+	H713_PQ_DCI,
+	H713_PQ_BLACK_EXTENSION,
+	H713_PQ_COUNT,
+};
+
+/**
+ * struct h713_hdmirx_pq - one picture setting, from V4L2 id to RPC
+ * @id: V4L2 control id
+ * @set: routine that writes the value
+ * @get: routine that reads it back
+ * @max: largest value the vendor data knows for this setting; the smallest
+ *	is 0 for all nine
+ * @preset: the value of the vendor's "standard" preset, used only when the
+ *	firmware cannot be asked
+ * @name: control name, for the private ids; NULL where V4L2 has the name
+ * @menu: menu entries, for the private ids; NULL makes it a plain integer
+ *
+ * The Set and Get routine names are hashed into component ids at run time
+ * by cpu_comm_name2id(); every one of them reproduces the id that the live
+ * routine table of the co-processor holds for it
+ * (mainline/docs/reference/cpu-comm-call-table.md, "Picture quality").
+ */
+struct h713_hdmirx_pq {
+	u32 id;
+	const char *set;
+	const char *get;
+	s32 max;
+	s32 preset;
+	const char *name;
+	const char * const *menu;
+};
+
+/*
+ * The Set handlers take their value as the first argument word and read
+ * nothing else; the Get handlers take no argument at all and answer with
+ * exactly one word, the value. Both shapes are read off the decompiled
+ * handlers in re/captures/weltneuheit/k5-pq-handler-20260907.log -- for
+ * brightness, contrast, saturation and hue the Get handler is in that log
+ * verbatim, for the other five it is the same 0x30 bytes in the same
+ * Set/Get pattern of the routine table but was not decompiled. That is why
+ * an answer outside the range below is discarded instead of used.
+ *
+ * The preset column is the "standard" row of [HDMI1] in the vendor's
+ * pq_picturemode.ini. For the last four it is also what the bring-up
+ * sequence above already sends (stock sessions 17 to 20), which is what
+ * h713_hdmirx_pq_read_all() cross-checks the readback against.
+ */
+static const struct h713_hdmirx_pq h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[H713_PQ_COUNT] = {
+	[H713_PQ_BRIGHTNESS] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_BRIGHTNESS,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetBrightness",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetBrightness",
+		.max = 100, .preset = 50,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_CONTRAST] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_CONTRAST,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetContrast",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetContrast",
+		.max = 100, .preset = 50,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_SATURATION] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_SATURATION,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetSaturation",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetSaturation",
+		.max = 100, .preset = 50,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_HUE] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_HUE,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetHue",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetHue",
+		.max = 100, .preset = 50,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_SHARPNESS] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_SHARPNESS,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetSharpness",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetSharpness",
+		.max = 100, .preset = 50,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_TNR] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_H713_TEMPORAL_NR,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetTNR",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetTNR",
+		.max = 3, .preset = 2,
+		.name = "Temporal Noise Reduction",
+		.menu = h713_hdmirx_pq_steps,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_SNR] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_H713_SPATIAL_NR,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetSNR",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetSNR",
+		.max = 3, .preset = 1,
+		.name = "Spatial Noise Reduction",
+		.menu = h713_hdmirx_pq_steps,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_DCI] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_H713_DYNAMIC_CONTRAST,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetDCI",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetDCI",
+		.max = 3, .preset = 2,
+		.name = "Dynamic Contrast",
+		.menu = h713_hdmirx_pq_steps,
+	},
+	[H713_PQ_BLACK_EXTENSION] = {
+		.id = V4L2_CID_H713_BLACK_EXTENSION,
+		.set = "THal_Vp_SetBlackExtension",
+		.get = "THal_Vp_GetBlackExtension",
+		.max = 3, .preset = 1,
+		.name = "Black Extension",
+		.menu = h713_hdmirx_pq_steps,
+	},
+};
+
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
 
 struct h713_hdmirx;
 
 /**
  * struct h713_hdmirx_slot_source - where "this slot is finished" comes from
@@ -398,12 +604,17 @@
 	phys_addr_t ring_size;
 
 	u32 id_setsource;
 
 	struct v4l2_dv_timings timings;
 
+	/* picture quality, one entry per h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[] entry */
+	struct v4l2_ctrl_handler ctrl_hdl;
+	s32 pq_start[H713_PQ_COUNT];
+	bool pq_from_fw[H713_PQ_COUNT];
+
 	/* slot bookkeeping, all of it under slot_lock */
 	spinlock_t slot_lock;
 	struct vb2_v4l2_buffer *slot_buf[H713_HDMIRX_SLOTS];
 	u32 last_flip_y;
 	u32 last_flip_c;
 	int last_slot;
@@ -574,12 +785,217 @@
 	u32 arg = H713_SOURCE_HDMI1;
 
 	return cpu_comm_call(rx->id_setsource, &arg, 1, NULL, 0, 0);
 }
 
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
+ * Picture quality: reading the firmware's state, and writing controls
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
+
+static const struct h713_hdmirx_pq *h713_hdmirx_pq_of(u32 id)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < H713_PQ_COUNT; i++)
+		if (h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[i].id == id)
+			return &h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[i];
+
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+/*
+ * What the bring-up sequence sent for this routine, or -1 if it does not
+ * send it. Read out of the sequence table rather than written down a second
+ * time, so the two cannot drift apart.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_init_arg(const char *name)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(h713_hdmirx_init_seq); i++) {
+		const struct h713_hdmirx_step *s = &h713_hdmirx_init_seq[i];
+
+		if (s->kind == H713_STEP_PLAIN && s->nargs == 1 &&
+		    !strcmp(s->name, name))
+			return s->args[0];
+	}
+
+	return -1;
+}
+
+static int h713_hdmirx_pq_read(const struct h713_hdmirx_pq *pq, s32 *out)
+{
+	u32 id = cpu_comm_name2id(pq->get, CPU_COMM_CPU_MIPS);
+	u32 word;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!id)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_call(id, NULL, 0, &word, 1, 0);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	if (ret < 1)
+		return -ENODATA;
+	if (word > (u32)pq->max)
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	*out = word;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Ask the firmware what it currently has, once, right after the bring-up
+ * sequence. The answers become the controls' values *and* their defaults,
+ * which is the only way to have both without inventing a number: a control
+ * that announces 50 while the firmware holds something else is a lie, and
+ * writing 50 to make the announcement true would move the picture away from
+ * the state the firmware boots in. Saturation is the sharp end of that --
+ * its RPC also sets the chroma gain, so writing back a value that was only
+ * assumed would change the colour of a picture nobody asked to change.
+ *
+ * The bring-up sequence has just set TNR, SNR, DCI and the black extension
+ * to known values, so four of the nine answers can be checked against
+ * something. If they disagree, the reading of the Get routines is wrong and
+ * the log says so -- that is the point of the comparison, and it can fail.
+ *
+ * A routine that does not answer at all ends the round: the co-processor is
+ * then either gone or busy, and eight more timeouts would only add four
+ * seconds to a probe that already carries the ten second hotplug pulse.
+ */
+static void h713_hdmirx_pq_read_all(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	unsigned int checked = 0, agreed = 0, got = 0;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < H713_PQ_COUNT; i++)
+		rx->pq_start[i] = h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[i].preset;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < H713_PQ_COUNT; i++) {
+		const struct h713_hdmirx_pq *pq = &h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[i];
+		int expect;
+		s32 val;
+		int ret;
+
+		ret = h713_hdmirx_pq_read(pq, &val);
+		if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT) {
+			dev_warn(rx->dev,
+				 "%s ohne Antwort -- die uebrigen Bildwerte werden nicht abgefragt\n",
+				 pq->get);
+			break;
+		}
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_info(rx->dev,
+				 "%s nicht verwertbar (%d), Vorgabe %d aus dem Stock-Preset\n",
+				 pq->get, ret, pq->preset);
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		rx->pq_start[i] = val;
+		rx->pq_from_fw[i] = true;
+		got++;
+
+		expect = h713_hdmirx_init_arg(pq->set);
+		if (expect < 0)
+			continue;
+
+		checked++;
+		if (val == expect) {
+			agreed++;
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		dev_warn(rx->dev,
+			 "%s liefert %d, die Init-Sequenz hat %d gesetzt\n",
+			 pq->get, val, expect);
+	}
+
+	dev_info(rx->dev,
+		 "Bildwerte: %u von %u aus der Firmware gelesen, %u von %u gegen die Init-Sequenz bestaetigt\n",
+		 got, H713_PQ_COUNT, agreed, checked);
+}
+
+/*
+ * One control, one RPC, no conversion -- see the block comment at the
+ * table. A failing RPC makes the ioctl fail; the control framework then
+ * keeps the old value, which is the truth as long as nobody can say what
+ * the firmware did with the half-finished call.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_s_ctrl(struct v4l2_ctrl *ctrl)
+{
+	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = container_of(ctrl->handler, struct h713_hdmirx,
+					      ctrl_hdl);
+	const struct h713_hdmirx_pq *pq = h713_hdmirx_pq_of(ctrl->id);
+	u32 arg;
+
+	if (!pq)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	arg = ctrl->val;
+
+	return h713_hdmirx_call(rx, pq->set, &arg, 1, 0);
+}
+
+static const struct v4l2_ctrl_ops h713_hdmirx_ctrl_ops = {
+	.s_ctrl = h713_hdmirx_s_ctrl,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The handler keeps its own lock rather than borrowing rx->lock: the video
+ * node already holds rx->lock across every ioctl, so sharing it would mean
+ * taking it twice. Nothing needs the two to be one lock -- a picture
+ * setting is independent of the queue, and cpu_comm serialises the calls
+ * themselves.
+ *
+ * v4l2_ctrl_handler_setup() is deliberately not called here. It would write
+ * all nine values into the firmware at probe, which is exactly what the
+ * readback exists to avoid.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_init_ctrls(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	struct v4l2_ctrl_handler *hdl = &rx->ctrl_hdl;
+	unsigned int i;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = v4l2_ctrl_handler_init(hdl, H713_PQ_COUNT);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < H713_PQ_COUNT; i++) {
+		const struct h713_hdmirx_pq *pq = &h713_hdmirx_pq_ctrls[i];
+
+		if (!pq->menu) {
+			v4l2_ctrl_new_std(hdl, &h713_hdmirx_ctrl_ops, pq->id,
+					  0, pq->max, 1, rx->pq_start[i]);
+		} else {
+			struct v4l2_ctrl_config cfg = {
+				.ops	= &h713_hdmirx_ctrl_ops,
+				.id	= pq->id,
+				.name	= pq->name,
+				.type	= V4L2_CTRL_TYPE_MENU,
+				.min	= 0,
+				.max	= pq->max,
+				.def	= rx->pq_start[i],
+				.qmenu	= pq->menu,
+			};
+
+			v4l2_ctrl_new_custom(hdl, &cfg, NULL);
+		}
